¶
'''yieldingly''' (yēl′ding-li), ''adv.'' In a yielding manner; with compliance.

'''yieldingness''' (yēl′ding-nes), ''n.'' The state or property of being yielding; disposition to comply.
{{smaller block|Bismarck wrote, there was only “one voice of regret on the subject in the Federal Assembly,” which in the opinion of many “had given itself a death-blow by its ''yieldingness'' in the question of Holstein.”
{{right|''Lowe'', Bismarck, I. 225.}}}}

'''yieldless†''' (yēld′les), ''a.'' [< ''yield'' + ''-less''.] Unyielding.
{{smaller block|Undaunted, ''yieldless'', firm. {{right|''Rowe'', Ulysses, iii.}}}}

'''yif†''', ''conj.'' An obsolete form of ''if''.

'''yill''' (yėl), ''n.'' A Scotch form of ''ale''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her bread it ’s to bake,
Her ''yill'' is to brew.</poem>
{{right|''Bonnie Earl o’ Murry'' (Child’s Ballads, VII. 122).}}
The clachan ''yill'' had made me canty.
{{right|''Burns'', Death and Dr. Hornbook.}}}}

