¶
'''yodel, yodle''' (yō′dl), ''n.'' [< ''yodel, v.''] A song or refrain in which there are frequent changes from the ordinary voice to a falsetto. Also sometimes called ''warble.''

'''yodeler, yodler''' (yō′del-ėr, -dlėr), ''n.'' One who sings yodels. Also ''yodeller.''

'''yof†''', ''conj.'' An obsolete dialectal variant of ''though.''
{{smaller block|My-selffe ''yof'' I saye itt. {{right|''York Plays'', p. 272.}}}}

'''yoga''' (yō′gä̤), ''n.'' [Hind. ''yoga'', < Skt. ''yoga'', union, devotion, < √ ''yuj'', join: see ''yoke''<sup>1</sup>.] One of the branches of the Hindu philosophy, which teaches the doctrines of the Supreme Being, and explains the means by which the human soul may obtain final emancipation from further migrations, and effect a junction with the universal spirit. {{smaller|Among the means of effecting this junction are comprehended a long continuance in various unnatural postures, withdrawal of the senses from external objects, concentration of the mind on some grand central truth, and the like, all of which imply the leading of an austere hermit life.}}

'''yogi''' (yō′gi), ''n.'' [Hind. ''yogī'', < ''yoga'': see ''yoga.''] A Hindu ascetic and mendicant who practises the yoga system, and combines meditation with austerity, claiming thus to acquire a miraculous power over elementary matter. See ''yoga.'' Also ''yogee'' and ''jogi.''
{{smaller block|Then Rawunna, the giant, assuming the shape of a pilgrim ''Yogee'' rolling to the caves of Ellora — with Gayntree the mystical text on his lips and the shadow of Siva’s beard in his soul — rolls to Rama’s door, and cries “Alms! alms!” {{right|''J. W. Palmer'', The New and the Old, p. 316.}}}}

'''yogism''' (yō′gizm), ''n.'' [< ''yoga'' + ''-ism.''] The doctrine and practices of the yogis; yoga.
